      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "So, once you're done with your intense workout, the lactic acid needs to be cleared out of your muscles. This process is a bit like a cleanup crew, and it's led by a team of enzymes that work together to break down the lactic acid and remove it from the muscles.\n\nOne of the main enzymes involved in this process is lactate dehydrogenase, which is the same enzyme that produced the lactic acid in the first place. This enzyme is found in the muscles, and it helps to convert the lactic acid back into pyruvate, which can then be used for energy production or excreted.\n\nAnother enzyme that plays a role in clearing out lactic acid is alanine transaminase. This enzyme helps to convert the lactic acid into alanine, which can then be transported to the liver for further processing.\n\nThe lactic acid is also cleared out of the muscles through a process called diffusion, where it simply leaks out of the muscle cells and into the bloodstream. This process is facilitated by the presence of certain ions, such as potassium and sodium, which help to drive the lactic acid out of the muscles.\n\nSo, to answer your question, the lactic acid doesn't just stay",
